  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 446 PM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nDry conditions through Monday will slowly begin to degrade as we \nmove into the mid-week period. A mid-level ridge and surface high \npressure over the Southeast begins to shift the low level moisture \nadvection over Southern Florida. Tuesday, the associated surface \nhigh-pressure system slides southeast into the Atlantic waters and \nbegins to shift surface winds to a southerly direction which will \nbegin to advect moisture from the south. This weak south-\nsouthwesterly surface flow trailing the eastward moving high \npressure system is forecast to scoop up only a modest level of \ntropical moisture from the Gulf and Caribbean Sea as precipitable\nwater values will remain near normal (1.45 inches) for this time \nof year by Tuesday afternoon. The mid-level ridging will help to \nstifle convection Tuesday with little in the way of mid to upper-\nlevel forcing, residual dry air aloft, and meager instability. \nThis will result in precipitation primarily being restricted to a \nfew showers developing along maximized ascent on the diurnally \ndriven sea/gulf breezes across the southern end of South Florida \nwhere low level boundary layer moisture levels remains the \nhighest. \n\nContinued moisture advection from the south-southwest on Wednesday \nwill raise precipitable water values further with ensemble guidance \nplacing the 50th percentile at 1.67 inches, which would be \nslightly above average for this time of year. 500mb temperatures \non Wednesday fall below -9C, which will act to steepen lapse \nrates, aiding in the development of thunderstorm activity as \ninstability increases. A jet streak moving off the Texas gulf \ncoast will add some extra forcing into the mix by late Wednesday \nso we could see some brief thunderstorm activity late in the day. \nThe surface winds coming from the southwest will be light allowing\nfor a sea breeze to push in from the Atlantic and pin the shower \nand thunderstorm activity along the eastern metros. Overall, rain \nchances Wednesday will elevate compared to Tuesday, but guidance \nis hinting at another day of scattered showers and a few \nthunderstorms. \n\nLooking ahead to the rest of the week, the big story is the upcoming \ncold front that is forecast to impact South Florida starting Friday. \nThe Weather Prediction Center's cluster analysis for the event has \nnot yet converged on a single solution, with the two solutions \ndescribing the most amount of the variance being tied to the \ntrack of the eastward moving low pressure system. One solution, \nwhich is favored by the European ensemble, has the low pressure \nsystem strengthening and quickly moving up the coast as a \nNor'easter. This solution brings a cold front down through Florida\nmore quickly, and brings associated effects thursday evening. A \nsecond solution, more favored by the North American ensembles \n(GEFS, GEPS), has the surface low push further out into the \nAtlantic waters, and focuses impacts in Southern Florida on Friday\nmorning instead. Depending on the placement of surface high \npressure across the southeastern United States this upcoming \nweekend into early next week, surface wind flow will dictate just \nhow long the dry and cool airmass will remain in place across \nSouth Florida.",
